<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- iOS discovery-extension entitlements: one keychain group, and only so
     this half can say that it ran.

     This half declares the AID that ctkd polls with and refuses every
     createToken. It reads no prime, holds no card session and stores no
     credential, and it is granted nothing that would let it. The group
     below exists for exactly one write: the trace line in
     DiscoveryTokenDriver.

     That line is worth the grant. An app extension's logging is invisible
     from outside on iOS 26 - `log stream --device` is gone and
     `log collect` fails - so "ctkd polled a card with our AID" is
     otherwise unobservable, and a login that failed before discovery is
     indistinguishable from one that failed at the mint. The trace is the
     only channel the three processes share.

     The grant is the app's own group, which is also the minting
     extension's, so this binary could in principle read the primed
     identity too. It does not. The stronger defence would be a second,
     dedicated group, which cannot be addressed without hard-coding the
     team prefix in source - an item's access group must be named in full.
     The trade is recorded here rather than left to be discovered.

     The group must stay FIRST in the app's array in
     Config/RefineID-iOS.entitlements: an item added without an explicit
     kSecAttrAccessGroup lands in the first group of the writer's
     entitlement, so reordering that array silently moves every write out
     of reach of both extensions. -->
<dict>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)fi.refineid.ReFineID</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
